{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And it was, all souls, etc., and Joseph, etc.</b> One needs to know why it says ״וַיְהִי״ — an expression of distress — and if it is on account of the exile, this is not the place for it. Also, one needs to know the reason for his saying <b>all</b>. Further, why was it necessary to go back and inform us of the number of those who emerged from Jacob's thigh, when He had already made it known to us and we knew them. Further, what novelty is this information — ״Joseph was in Egypt״?" "<b>Or perhaps</b> the verse intends to say, along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Sotah 36a), that Joseph was destined to establish twelve tribes, and what caused that he established only two? It was the evil encounter that befell him — the wife of Potiphar — ״וַיָּפֹזּוּ זְרֹעֵי יָדָיו״ (Genesis 49:24), end of quote. From this you learn that but for this, Joseph would have established twelve tribes, and that is why it says <b>וַיְהִי</b> — an expression of distress — for the Torah grieves that among all of them there were only seventy souls. And that is why it says <b>all souls</b>, etc., and it gives the reason for the matter — that there were only seventy — and says <b>and Joseph was</b>, etc. And this is the cause that those who emerged from Jacob's thigh were only seventy souls, for had Joseph not gone down to Egypt, Joseph would have established twelve tribes and the sons of Jacob would have been eighty branches. And through this all the fine points are resolved together." "<b>Further</b>, the verse intends by saying <b>all souls</b>, etc., <b>and Joseph</b>, etc., to say that the totality of all these branches are equal in goodness with no distinction among them — all are righteous, all are wise, all are with a whole heart toward one another, as if all of them were a single soul. And it informs us of a novelty and a great superiority on Joseph's part, that he was in Egypt, and by nature he would have changed from the equality of the brothers — both in the matter of brotherhood, for it is impossible that he would be equal in affection to the equality of all of them, given what they did to him in selling him and acting cruelly toward him, so how could there not be some change between him and all of them; and in the matter of righteousness, for he was placed in the crucible of impurity while the brothers were in the land of Canaan sheltered under the tree, which is Jacob their father — and in any case he would have changed somewhat. Even so, the verse informs you that he is equal to them and enters into the count of the seventy, and equal to all of them together to be called a single soul — both to specify the love and brotherhood that is proper between them, and in the matter of righteousness and purity of soul, that no disease of the soul clung to him despite his being in Egypt. And see what I wrote on the verse (Genesis 46:30) ״אָמוּתָה הַפַּעַם״, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:23:11.124777086Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}